WEBVTT

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:20.550
<v Ira Glass>I've known Kristy a few years. And she's been going through a rough time. When her boyfriend dumped her, she wanted to do something. She wasn't over him. And she had all these feelings. And it's hard to just sit there. And she thought about standing outside of his window and serenading him. She's a singer. That's her job. She sings. But she actually had tried serenading a guy once.
<v Ira Glass>我和克里斯蒂认识几年了。她最近过得很艰难。当她的男朋友甩了她之后，她想做点什么。她还没放下他。她心里有各种各样的情绪。干坐着实在太难受了。她想过站在他窗外为他唱小夜曲。她是名歌手，那是她的工作，她靠唱歌为生。但其实她以前试过给一个男人唱小夜曲。

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:40.230
<v Kristy Kruger>That really didn't work out. I would like to say, as a woman, I believe there is a serenade double standard. Where it's kind of OK for a man to go throw a rock at a window, but when a woman does it, it's sort of like, call the police. There's a singer/songwriter on my lawn.
<v Kristy Kruger>那次真的很失败。作为女性，我想说，我认为小夜曲存在双重标准。男人往窗户扔石头似乎没问题，但如果是女人这么做，就像是——“快报警，我草坪上有个创作歌手”。

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.180
<v Ira Glass>Kind of like you're a crazy person.
<v Ira Glass>就像你是疯子一样。

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:44.360
<v Kristy Kruger>Yeah.
<v Kristy Kruger>是的。

00:00:44.360 --> 00:01:10.720
<v Ira Glass>And she didn't want to be a crazy person, right? She didn't want to stalk him. She didn't want to bother him. But like I say, she had all these feelings. So she wrote him a letter every day that she did not send. And she started collecting stuff that she knew that he would like. And she kept it. She just kept it in a box underneath her bed. She's also got food for him in her freezer, these pastries that he likes that she saw and couldn't help buying for him.
<v Ira Glass>她不想当个疯子，对吧？她不想跟踪他，也不想打扰他。但正如我所说，她心里有这些情绪。于是，她每天都写信给他，但从不寄出。她开始收集她知道他会喜欢的东西，并把它们存起来。她把这些东西塞在床底下的盒子里。她甚至还在冷冻库里给他准备了食物，那是他喜欢吃的糕点，她看到了就忍不住买给他。

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:18.180
<v Ira Glass>And she knows that he doesn't want to get any of this stuff from her. So what is she collecting this stuff for? She has no idea.
<v Ira Glass>她知道他根本不想收到这些东西。那她收集这些东西是为了什么？她自己也不知道。

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.620
<v Ira Glass>Finally, she figured out a way to reach out to him that she thought might be appropriate.
<v Ira Glass>最后，她想出了一个她认为可能比较合适的联系他的方法。

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.220
<v Kristy Kruger>He was a prankster. He loved pranks.
<v Kristy Kruger>他是个爱恶作剧的人，他很喜欢恶作剧。

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:36.730
<v Ira Glass>Including phone pranks. And so Kristy explained to me that as she would drive around the country from one music gig to the next, she tried to do something that she thought would talk to that part of his personality.
<v Ira Glass>包括电话恶作剧。克里斯蒂向我解释说，当她开车穿梭于全国各地的演出地点时，她尝试做一些她认为能触动他那种个性的事情。

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:45.160
<v Kristy Kruger>I started writing his number on bathroom walls across the United States.
<v Kristy Kruger>我开始在全美的厕所墙上写下他的号码。

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.140
<v Ira Glass>What?
<v Ira Glass>什么？

00:01:46.140 --> 00:02:02.350
<v Kristy Kruger>Yeah, I started writing on bathroom walls, "Ladies, please help me out. I really want my man back. Please call--" and then I put his number. "And tell him his chalupa really misses him."
<v Kristy Kruger>是啊，我开始在厕所墙上写：“女士们，请帮帮我。我真的很想挽回我的男人。请致电……”然后我写下他的号码。“告诉他，他的卷饼真的很想他。”

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:05.270
<v Kristy Kruger>And then I wrote, "Say your city and state, please."
<v Kristy Kruger>然后我又写道：“请说出你们的城市和州。”

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:12.910
<v Ira Glass>I see. Because you knew that he doesn't want to see you, so you don't want to be calling him. That would be clearly out of bounds.
<v Ira Glass>我明白了。因为你知道他不想见到你，所以你不想直接打电话给他。那显然是越界了。

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:19.440
<v Kristy Kruger>Right. That's how I cleared myself of that, by having other women call and text him.
<v Kristy Kruger>没错。这就是我开脱的方法，让别的女人去给他打电话、发短信。

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:25.080
<v Ira Glass>Is it possible, though, if he doesn't want to hear from you, that this is even worse?
<v Ira Glass>不过，如果他不想听你的消息，这样做会不会更糟？

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:40.560
<v Kristy Kruger>Well, and that's eventually what happened. Two months later, I received a very cold, dry text. "I hope you're doing OK. But please knock it off with the bathroom walls. Desist with this."
<v Kristy Kruger>嗯，最后发生的就是那样。两个月后，我收到了一条非常冰冷、生硬的短信。“我希望你一切都好。但请别再在厕所墙上乱写了。停止这种行为吧。”

00:02:40.560 --> 00:03:01.970
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. So Kristy tried a more constructive route. She started to learn these sad, old country songs, Floyd Tillman and Hank Williams. And she formed a cover band to play them that she calls, The Town Criers-- get it? Town Criers-- in Denton, Texas, where she lives. I asked her to send me one of those songs, and they are really, really sad.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。所以克里斯蒂尝试了一条更具建设性的路线。她开始学习那些悲伤的乡村老歌，比如弗洛伊德·蒂尔曼和汉克·威廉姆斯的歌。她在居住的德克萨斯州丹顿市组建了一支翻唱乐队，取名叫“城镇呐喊者”——懂了吗？城镇呐喊者。我让她寄给我一首，那些歌真的、真的很悲伤。

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:17.350
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐响起]

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:30.110
<v Ira Glass>All of the stuff that Kristy has done since the breakup-- recording this music, playing the prank, buying all that stuff for her boyfriend-- she says she just can't help herself. She still has feelings, and she just has to act.
<v Ira Glass>分手后，克里斯蒂所做的这一切——录制这些音乐、玩恶作剧、为前男友买那些东西——她说她只是无法克制自己。她仍然有感情，她必须采取行动。

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:43.120
<v Kristy Kruger>I was raised that you can always get what you want out of life, you just have to work hard. And my dad never really explained to me that that didn't apply to love.
<v Kristy Kruger>从小我受的教育是：你总能得到你想要的生活，只要努力工作就行。但我爸爸从没跟我解释过，这套逻辑不适用于爱情。

00:03:43.120 --> 00:04:34.650
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio program, we hear people try all kinds of things, work hard in all kinds of ways for love. Including, I have to say, a few ways that I bet you have never heard of. And they do it because they just can not stop themselves. Like every country song ever written would tell you, they've got no choice. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. It's a special Valentine's Day edition of our show, "What I Did For Love." I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us. Kristy Kruger.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中，我们听到人们尝试各种方法，为了爱情以各种方式努力。我得说，甚至包括一些你肯定闻所未闻的方法。他们这样做是因为他们真的无法控制自己。就像每一首乡村歌曲所唱的那样，他们别无选择。来自芝加哥WBEZ，这是《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。这是我们节目的情人节特别版，“我为爱所做的一切”。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。克里斯蒂·克鲁格。

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.520
<v Ira Glass>Act One, The Best Laid Plans.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，最好的计划。

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:48.080
<v Ira Glass>When you hear what this first couple did for love, I think one of the things that is most incredible about it is the kind of couple they were when they did it, which is a really great couple.
<v Ira Glass>当你听到这对情侣为爱所做的一切时，我认为最不可思议的事情之一，是他们在做这些事时作为情侣的状态——他们曾是一对非常棒的情侣。

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:53.010
<v Kurt Braunohler>We were always like the most solid of all the relationships of our friends and everything like that.
<v Kurt Braunohler>在我们所有的朋友中，我们的关系一直是最稳固的，诸如此类。

00:04:53.010 --> 00:05:08.990
<v Ira Glass>Kurt Braunohler met his girlfriend on the third day of college. And in all of the ways that we think of what makes for a good relationship, I think that they were doing better than you, or me, or most people. They had a lot of fun together. They could talk about anything. They didn't really fight. Happy sex life.
<v Ira Glass>库尔特·布劳诺勒在大学的第三天就遇到了他的女朋友。在我们认为什么是好关系的所有标准上，我认为他们比你、我或者大多数人都做得更好。他们在一起玩得很开心。他们无话不谈。他们几乎不吵架。性生活也很美满。

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:26.910
<v Kurt Braunohler>We really just got along very well together. We traveled well together. We were the place that friends would come when they are having hard times, and they would stay with us. It felt like very adult. Even when I was 23, it felt like, I'm kind of married, even though neither of us had ever, ever discussed getting married.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我们相处得非常融洽。我们一起旅行很合拍。当朋友们遇到困难时，他们会来找我们，住在我们这里。那种感觉非常成熟。即使我在23岁时，也觉得：我好像已经结婚了，尽管我们从来没有讨论过结婚的事。

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:28.670
<v Ira Glass>So what happened?
<v Ira Glass>那么发生了什么？

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:51.340
<v Kurt Braunohler>Well, after we had both turned 30, one day I just kind of was thinking about why we had never talked about getting married. We had never, ever talked about it, seriously or otherwise. And whenever anyone would ask us, we would always just kind of brush it off and say, oh, we'll get married when we have kids. That's what we would always say.
<v Kurt Braunohler>嗯，在我们都过了30岁之后，有一天我突然在想，为什么我们从来没谈过结婚的事。我们从来没有认真地或是随便地讨论过它。每当有人问起我们时，我们总是会搪塞过去，说“哦，等我们要孩子的时候再结婚”。我们总是这么说。

00:05:51.340 --> 00:06:19.670
<v Kurt Braunohler>And I remember, we were sitting in the living room and it was October. And I just said, hey, I want to talk. And I said, why do you think we haven't gotten married yet, or even talked about it? And she just kind of like looked at me and thought for a second. And then she said, well, I think that before we get married, we should probably sleep with other people.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我记得那时是十月，我们正坐在客厅里。我直接说：“嘿，我想谈谈。”我问她：“你觉得为什么我们还没结婚，甚至没谈过这件事？”她看着我，思考了一会儿。然后她说：“嗯，我觉得在结婚之前，我们或许应该和别人睡过才对。”

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:33.100
<v Kurt Braunohler>And you would imagine that that would come as a huge shock to me, like hit me hard. But for some reason it made sense to me. I was like, OK, very calmly. And I guess the reason--
<v Kurt Braunohler>你可能会以为这会让我大受震惊，重重地击中我。但不知为何，这对我来说很合理。我很冷静地说：“好吧。”我想原因在于——

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:36.440
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. How did that make sense?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。这怎么会合理呢？

00:06:36.440 --> 00:07:04.510
<v Kurt Braunohler>I've thought a lot about this. And I think that there's a few reasons. We both had this kind of arrogant notion of our relationship, that it could survive literally anything. We had known each other our whole adult lives. We were each other's world. So really, I don't think we thought that we could destroy this thing.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我仔细想过这件事。我认为有几个原因。我们对自己关系的看法有点傲慢，觉得它几乎能挺过任何事情。我们度过了彼此的整个成年时光，我们就是彼此的世界。所以，真的，我不认为我们当时觉得我们会毁掉这段关系。

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:28.420
<v Ira Glass>I talked to the girlfriend who did not want to come on the radio. And she said, basically, that she had only had two boyfriends in her life, her high school boyfriend, who was not a good boyfriend at all, and Kurt, who was great. But she met Kurt, she said, when she was 17. She was 30 when this happened. And she said that she felt like maybe she missed out on something in her twenties, experiences that other people had. And she didn't want to regret that.
<v Ira Glass>我和那位不想上广播的女友聊过。她基本上说，她一生中只交过两个男朋友，一个是高中时的男友，那个人一点都不好；另一个就是库尔特，他很棒。但她说，她17岁遇到库尔特时，发生这件事时她已经30岁了。她说她觉得或许自己在二十多岁时错过了一些东西，错过了别人经历过的那些事，她不想对此感到遗憾。

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:36.700
<v Ira Glass>A warning, I should say, before we go any further in this story. We're going to acknowledge the existence of sex between adults. Nothing explicit.
<v Ira Glass>我得提醒一下，在深入故事之前，我们要承认成年人之间存在性行为。没有露骨的描述。

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:39.440
<v Ira Glass>So they sat on the couch, and they talked this through.
<v Ira Glass>于是他们坐在沙发上，把这件事商量清楚了。

00:07:39.440 --> 00:08:11.830
<v Kurt Braunohler>The next part of that conversation was the logistics. Do we break up? Do I move out? Do we just do this while we're living in the same house together? And we kind of came up with this idea of borrowing this Amish concept called rumspringa. And rumspringa in the Amish world is when you're 16, you're allowed to be not Amish for two years. And then when you turn 18, you decide whether or not you come back to the fold.
<v Kurt Braunohler>谈话的下一部分是后勤安排。我们要分手吗？我搬出去吗？我们要住在一起时这么做吗？我们想出了一个借用阿米什人概念的主意，叫“阿米什青年出走期”（Rumspringa）。在阿米什人的世界里，当你16岁时，你可以被允许两年不当阿米什人。等到18岁时，你再决定是否回到群体中。

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:15.670
<v Ira Glass>And during those two years, kids get drunk. They sleep around. They try drugs.
<v Ira Glass>在那两年里，孩子们会喝醉。他们会到处乱睡。他们会尝试毒品。

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:16.540
<v Kurt Braunohler>They do lots of meth.
<v Kurt Braunohler>他们会吸很多冰毒。

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:18.140
<v Ira Glass>They do lots of stuff.
<v Ira Glass>他们会做很多事。

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:32.880
<v Kurt Braunohler>So we decided to have a rumspringa from our relationship. And that's the other crazy thing is that we decided that 30 days was enough. Because probably within 30 days, sleeping with other people, we'll get that all out of our system in 30 days.
<v Kurt Braunohler>所以我们决定在我们的关系中也来一次“出走期”。疯狂的是，我们觉得30天就够了。因为可能不出30天，通过和别人上床，我们就能把这些都从系统里清理出去了。

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:52.580
<v Kurt Braunohler>It turns out, it's really difficult to all of a sudden become single at 31 when you've never been single for your entire life. Because all of a sudden, I'm in New York City, and I'm single. And I essentially have the emotional tools of a 17-year-old boy.
<v Kurt Braunohler>事实证明，在31岁时突然变成单身，而你这一生从没单身过，这是非常困难的。因为突然之间，我在纽约，我是单身。而且我基本上只有17岁男孩的情感工具。

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:55.220
<v Ira Glass>Because the last time you had dated, you were 17.
<v Ira Glass>因为你上次约会时才17岁。

00:08:55.220 --> 00:09:28.330
<v Kurt Braunohler>The last time I dated I was in a dorm room. Also, it wasn't like I was single in New York City. Because being single in New York City is, I think, the majority of it is being just very lonely and being on your own. Whereas this was very different. I was in a race. I was in a 30-day race to sleep with as many people as I possibly could. Because after 30 days, I was going to go back and then get married.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我上次约会是在宿舍里。而且，这不像是在纽约市做单身汉。因为在纽约做单身汉，我认为大部分时间是感到非常孤独和独自一人。而这次完全不同。我在参加一场比赛。我在一场30天的比赛中，尽可能多地与人上床。因为30天后，我就要回去结婚了。

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:30.100
<v Ira Glass>So there's a time clock ticking.
<v Ira Glass>所以有一个倒计时钟在走。

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:45.080
<v Kurt Braunohler>Every single-- it was almost to the minute where I'd just be like, if I wasn't out somewhere trying to meet someone, I was like, this is wasted time. I am wasting my time right now.
<v Kurt Braunohler>每一个……几乎每一分钟我都在想，如果我没出门去见某人，我就会觉得：这是在浪费时间。我此刻正在浪费我的时间。

00:09:45.080 --> 00:10:03.260
<v Kurt Braunohler>And also, because of the fact that we were both-- my girlfriend and I were both very competitive with each other that we didn't speak during that 30 days. But I think in both of our minds, we had a competitive nature of being like, I need to sleep with more people than she does. And I think she felt the same way.
<v Kurt Braunohler>而且，由于我和女朋友当时都非常好胜，在这30天里我们没有说话。但我认为在我们内心深处，都有种好胜心，想着：我要比她睡过更多的人。我想她也是这么想的。

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:06.120
<v Ira Glass>That's true. She did feel that way. I asked her.
<v Ira Glass>那是真的。她的确是那么想的。我问过她。

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:19.770
<v Ira Glass>So before they started the rumspringa, they took a last romantic trip together to New Orleans over New Years. And they had a great time. And then on January 3, after they both cried a bit, Kurt moved out for a month.
