<v Justin>So we were in class. And I sat right next to her. And she was like, hey, do you have that? And I was like, you know what? Yeah, I do. And I was like, OK, we're going to wait a few. Because I'm not going to be like, oh, hey, give it to her in her hand, slide it. But she was like, OK, put it in my purse. OK. She put her purse on top of  my desk. And then I slid it right in there.
<v Justin>所以我们在上课。我坐在她旁边。她说：“嘿，你有那个东西吗？”我说：“你知道吗？是的，我有。”我说：“好吧，我们要等一会儿。因为我不想表现得……哦，嘿，在手里给她，滑过去。”她说：“好的，把它放进我的钱包里。”好的。她把钱包放在我的课桌上。然后我就把它滑了进去。

00:33:00.480 --> 00:33:05.380
<v Justin>She goes, OK, now take the money. I was like, no, I don't need the money. It's OK. Just have it. You can take it.
<v Justin>她说：“好了，现在收下钱。”我说：“不，我不需要钱。没关系。留着吧。你可以拿着。”

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:26.200
<v Justin>She goes, no, please take the money. You make me feel bad. So 10 minutes passed. And then we're still arguing about it. I'm like, please. No, I don't want the money. And she goes, no, just take the money. Take the money. Please, take the money. It will make me feel better. I'm just like, OK, you know what? Just so you could just shut up, I'll take the money. Don't worry. You're making me feel bad, now that I'm taking the money from you.
<v Justin>她说：“不，请收下钱。你让我感觉很糟。”所以十分钟过去了。我们还在为这事争论。我说：“拜托。不，我不想要钱。”她说：“不，就收下钱吧。收下钱。拜托，收下钱。这会让我感觉好点。”我就想：“好吧，你知道吗？为了让你闭嘴，我收下钱。别担心。你让我感觉很糟，现在我从你那里收了钱。”

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:26.920
<v Robbie Brown>How much money?
<v Robbie Brown>多少钱？

00:33:26.920 --> 00:33:28.850
<v Justin>$25.
<v Justin>25美元。

00:33:28.850 --> 00:33:30.580
<v Robbie Brown>And this was at school?
<v Robbie Brown>这还是在学校里？

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:32.630
<v Justin>Yes.
<v Justin>是的。

00:33:32.630 --> 00:33:50.710
<v Robbie Brown>Justin would later find out it's a felony in Florida to sell marijuana. And the penalty is even harsher for selling it on school property. In other words, Justin had made an irreversibly bad decision. The worst decision of his life, he says. And since he was over 18, he was legally an adult when he made it.
<v Robbie Brown>贾斯汀后来才知道在佛罗里达州贩卖大麻是重罪。在校区内贩卖的惩罚甚至更严厉。换句话说，贾斯汀做出了一个不可逆转的错误决定。这是他一生中最糟糕的决定，他说。而且因为他已经超过18岁，他在做这个决定时在法律上已经是成年人了。

00:33:50.710 --> 00:34:00.880
<v Robbie Brown>Finally, in May, the police did a big sweep. They arrested 31 people at several schools, almost all of them students who had sold small amounts of marijuana. Justin was one of them.
<v Robbie Brown>最终在五月，警察进行了一次大规模的搜捕。他们在几所学校逮捕了31人，几乎全都是卖过少量大麻的学生。贾斯汀就是其中之一。

00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:26.440
<v Justin>And they said that I had sold to the undercover cop. And I'm weirded out. I'm like, whoa. Who did I ever sell to? And then it just popped into my head that the only person that I ever did give anything to was this female. And I was shocked. My emotions were running. I was just like, oh my god. This is so mean. This is so messed up. How can she do this?
<v Justin>他们说我卖给了那个卧底警察。我很奇怪。我心想：“哇。我卖给过谁？”然后我脑子里突然冒出念头，我给过东西的唯一一个人就是这个女性。我惊呆了。我的情绪很激动。我心想：“哦，天哪。这太卑鄙了。这太糟糕了。她怎么能这么做？”

00:34:26.440 --> 00:34:58.930
<v Justin>I mean, if it was a guy doing this, if a guy ever tried to come up to me like this, I would have put my hand in his face straight up, and said, no, get out of my face. Please, get out of my face. I don't associate with that. But it's just a female. It's a different feeling that you get for a female than you would when you have your home boy or a guy come up to you and ask you a question like that. Oh, hey, do you smoke? No, no, I don't. Get out of here. It was a different feeling that when you get from a girl you like. You're not going to turn down the person that you want to be with.
<v Justin>我的意思是，如果是一个男人这么做，如果一个男人这样走近我，我会直接把手甩在他脸上，说：“不，离我远点。拜托，离我远点。我不做这种事。”但这只是个女性。比起你哥们或一个男人走过来问你这种问题，你对女性会产生不同的感觉。比如，“哦，嘿，你抽烟吗？”“不，不，我不抽。滚开。”那和你从你喜欢的女孩那里得到的感觉不同。你不会拒绝你想在一起的人。

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:06.760
<v Robbie Brown>When I talked to the officer who plays Naomi, she said she had no regrets about her undercover work. She's a cop. And she sees it the way a cop does.
<v Robbie Brown>当我采访扮演娜奥米的警官时，她说她对自己的卧底工作没有遗憾。她是警察。她以警察的方式看待这个问题。

00:35:06.760 --> 00:35:18.870
<v Naomi>These kids need to wake up. They need to realize they can't be doing this.
<v Naomi>这些孩子需要清醒过来。他们需要意识到他们不能做这种事。

00:35:18.870 --> 00:35:36.840
<v Robbie Brown>And using undercover officers has gotten results. The police in Palm Beach County tried the same tactics again at another group of schools last semester. So far, they've arrested more than 80 people. All have pled guilty to charges ranging from selling cocaine and pills to bringing weapons on school property.
<v Robbie Brown>使用卧底警察已经取得了成果。棕榈滩县的警察在上学期在另一组学校尝试了同样的策略。到目前为止，他们已经逮捕了80多人。所有人都认罪了，罪名从贩卖可卡因和药片到在学校携带武器不等。

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:48.510
<v Robbie Brown>Justin took a plea deal, three years probation in exchange for pleading guilty to the felony charge. His lawyer told him that if he went to trial, it would be his word against Naomi's. And they disagreed about the drug deal.
<v Robbie Brown>贾斯汀达成了认罪协议，以对重罪认罪换取三年缓刑。他的律师告诉他，如果他上法庭，那将是他对娜奥米的一面之词。而且他们对毒品交易有分歧。

00:35:48.510 --> 00:36:07.670
<v Robbie Brown>He says she kept asking him to sell her marijuana. She says he brought it up first. He says she insisted he take money for it. She says he just took the money without prompting. Either way, the police had Justin's text messages telling Naomi he would get marijuana for her. So Justin's lawyer advised him, just take the plea.
<v Robbie Brown>他说她一直求他卖大麻给她。她说是他先提出来的。他说她坚持让他收钱。她说他只是在没有催促的情况下就收了钱。无论如何，警方有贾斯汀发给娜奥米的短信，说他会为她搞到大麻。所以贾斯汀的律师建议他，接受认罪协议吧。

00:36:07.670 --> 00:36:16.320
<v Robbie Brown>But before the deal came through, he spent more than a week in jail going over and over in his mind what had happened. And still thinking, in spite of himself, about Naomi.
<v Robbie Brown>但在协议达成之前，他在监狱里待了一周多，在脑海里反复重演发生的一切。尽管他不想，但他仍然在想娜奥米。

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:29.240
<v Justin>I would have a good time when I would be with her. And I would be with her all the time. And that's why, when all this collapsed and caved in, I felt so-- it just hurt.
<v Justin>我和她在一起时很开心。我几乎一直和她在一起。这就是为什么当这一切崩塌时，我感觉如此……它只是伤人。

00:36:29.240 --> 00:36:32.410
<v Robbie Brown>Have you had any contact with Naomi--
<v Robbie Brown>你和娜奥米有什么联系吗——

00:36:32.410 --> 00:36:32.740
<v Justin>No, not--
<v Justin>不，没有——

00:36:32.740 --> 00:36:33.660
<v Robbie Brown>--since you were arrested?
<v Robbie Brown>——自从你被捕以来？

00:36:33.660 --> 00:36:33.910
<v Justin>Not at all.
<v Justin>完全没有。

00:36:33.910 --> 00:36:35.440
<v Robbie Brown>Have you tried to send her any messages?
<v Robbie Brown>你有没有试过给她发任何信息？

00:36:35.440 --> 00:36:42.000
<v Justin>No. I don't have any contact with her whatsoever. If I would, I would love to have a conversation with her. No disrespect or anything.
<v Justin>没有。我跟她完全没有任何联系。如果可以的话，我很想和她谈谈。没有不敬之类的。

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:42.900
<v Robbie Brown>What would you tell her?
<v Robbie Brown>你会告诉她什么？

00:36:42.900 --> 00:36:50.570
<v Justin>I would tell her, what the heck did she do?
<v Justin>我会告诉她，她到底干了什么？

00:36:50.570 --> 00:37:04.120
<v Robbie Brown>Justin had planned to go into the Air Force after high school. He said he wanted to make something out of his life. And now, with a felony conviction, the Armed Forces, any part of it, was off the table. Justin is applying to community college.
<v Robbie Brown>贾斯汀原计划高中毕业后参军。他说他想在生活中闯出点名堂。而现在，有了重罪记录，武装部队，无论哪一个部门，都与他无缘了。贾斯汀正在申请社区大学。

00:37:04.120 --> 00:37:25.100
<v Robbie Brown>He says now the whole thing seems kind of unbelievable. He was an honor roll student, a first time offender with no criminal record. And of all the high schools in all the towns in all the world, she walked into his.
<v Robbie Brown>他说现在整件事看起来有点不可思议。他曾是一名荣誉生，是一个没有犯罪记录的初犯。而在全世界所有城镇的所有高中里，她偏偏走进了他的那一所。

00:37:25.100 --> 00:37:30.930
<v Ira Glass>Robbie Brown works for the the New York Times in Atlanta.
<v Ira Glass>罗比·布朗为亚特兰大的《纽约时报》工作。

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:51.770
<v Ira Glass>Act 3, Cold Stone Dreamery. This next story, like that last one we just heard, is about a guy who yearns for someone, yearns. And this someone is not giving him much back. And this someone may not actually be good for him at all. Except in this story, the guy is a duck. This story is from Ben Loory.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，冷石梦境。下一个故事，就像我们刚才听到的上一个故事一样，是关于一个渴望某人的家伙，极度渴望。而这个人并没有给他太多回应。而这个人可能根本不适合他。只不过在这个故事里，这个家伙是一只鸭子。这个故事来自本·卢瑞。

00:37:51.770 --> 00:38:02.140
<v Ben Loory>A duck fell in love with a rock. It was a large rock, about the size of a duck actually, that was situated off the bank of the river, a little past the old elm.
<v Ben Loory>一只鸭子爱上了一块石头。那是一块大石头，大约有一只鸭子那么大，位于河岸边，就在那棵老榆树过去一点点的地方。

00:38:02.140 --> 00:38:06.700
<v Ben Loory>Every day after lunch, the duck would saunter off to admire the rock for a while.
<v Ben Loory>每天午饭后，那只鸭子就会溜过去，欣赏一会儿石头。

00:38:06.700 --> 00:38:09.420
<v Ben Loory>"Where are you going?" said the other ducks.
<v Ben Loory>“你要去哪儿？”其他鸭子说。

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:12.860
<v Ben Loory>"Nowhere," said the duck. "Just around."
<v Ben Loory>“没去哪儿，”鸭子说。“就随便转转。”

00:38:12.860 --> 00:38:18.060
<v Ben Loory>But the other ducks knew exactly where he was going. And they all laughed at him behind his back.
<v Ben Loory>但其他鸭子非常清楚他要去哪儿。他们都在背后嘲笑他。

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:24.270
<v Ben Loory>"Stupid duck is in love with a rock," they snickered. "Wonder what kind of ducklings they will have."
<v Ben Loory>“那只傻鸭子爱上了一块石头，”他们窃笑道。“真想知道他们会生出什么样的小鸭子。”

00:38:24.270 --> 00:38:41.220
<v Ben Loory>But there was one duck, a girl duck, who did not laugh. She had known the strange duck for a long time and had always found him to be a good and decent bird. She felt sorry for him. It was hard luck to fall in love with a rock. She wanted to help, but what could she do?
<v Ben Loory>但有一只鸭子，一只母鸭子，没有笑。她认识这只奇怪的鸭子很长时间了，一直觉得他是一只优秀体面的鸟。她为他感到难过。爱上一块石头真是倒霉透顶。她想帮忙，但她能做什么呢？

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:45.540
<v Ben Loory>She trailed after the duck and watched him woo the rock from behind a tree.
<v Ben Loory>她跟在鸭子后面，从树后看着他向石头献殷勤。

00:38:45.540 --> 00:39:03.770
<v Ben Loory>"I love you," the duck was saying. "I love you. I love you. I love you. I love you more than the stars in the sky. I love you more than the fish in the river. I love you more than, more than--" And there he stopped for he could think of nothing else that existed.
<v Ben Loory>“我爱你，”鸭子在说。“我爱你。我爱你。我爱你。我爱你胜过天上的星星。我爱你胜过河里的鱼。我爱你胜过……胜过——”他说到这里停住了，因为他想不出还有什么别的东西存在。

00:39:03.770 --> 00:39:12.350
<v Ben Loory>"Life itself?" said the girl duck from behind the tree. She hadn't meant to pipe up. The words just sort of leapt out of her.
<v Ben Loory>“生命本身？”母鸭子从树后说。她没打算插嘴。这些话就那样脱口而出。

00:39:12.350 --> 00:39:16.340
<v Ben Loory>The duck spun around to look at her. He was terrified.
<v Ben Loory>鸭子猛地转过身看着她。他吓坏了。

00:39:16.340 --> 00:39:25.130
<v Ben Loory>"It's OK," said the girl duck, waddling out from behind the tree. "I know you're in love with the rock. In fact, everyone knows."
<v Ben Loory>“没关系，”母鸭子从树后摇摇摆摆地走出来说。“我知道你爱上了那块石头。事实上，大家都知道。”

00:39:25.130 --> 00:39:27.260
<v Ben Loory>"They do?" said the duck.
<v Ben Loory>“他们知道？”鸭子说。

00:39:27.260 --> 00:39:33.480
<v Ben Loory>"Yes," said the girl duck. "Yes, they do."
<v Ben Loory>“是的，”母鸭子说。“是的，他们知道。”

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:40.280
<v Ben Loory>The duck sighed and sat down on the ground. If he had had hands, he would have buried his head in them.
<v Ben Loory>鸭子叹了口气，坐在地上。如果他有手，他会把头埋在手里。

00:39:40.280 --> 00:39:45.340
<v Ben Loory>"What am I going to do?" he said. "What am I going to do?"
<v Ben Loory>“我该怎么办？”他说。“我该怎么办？”

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:47.740
<v Ben Loory>"Do?" the girl duck said.
<v Ben Loory>“怎么办？”母鸭子说。

00:39:47.740 --> 00:39:56.700
<v Ben Loory>"How can it go on like this?" Said the duck. "I love a thing that can not speak, can not move, can not-- I don't even know how it feels about me."
<v Ben Loory>“怎么能就这样继续下去？”鸭子说。“我爱着一个不能说话、不能移动、不能……我甚至不知道它对我的感觉。”

00:39:56.700 --> 00:40:01.000
<v Ben Loory>The girl duck looked at the rock. She didn't know what to say.
<v Ben Loory>母鸭子看着那块石头。她不知道该说什么。

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:13.620
<v Ben Loory>"I know," said the duck, "you think I'm crazy. You think it's just a rock. But it isn't just a rock. It's different. It's very different." He looked at the rock.
<v Ben Loory>“我知道，”鸭子说，“你以为我疯了。你以为那只是一块石头。但它不仅仅是一块石头。它是不同的。它非常不同。”他看着那块石头。

00:40:13.620 --> 00:40:25.200
<v Ben Loory>"But something has to happen," he said, "and soon. Because my heart will break if this goes on much longer."
<v Ben Loory>“但总得发生点什么，”他说，“而且要快。因为如果再这样下去，我的心会碎的。”

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:34.480
<v Ben Loory>That night, the girl duck had a hard time sleeping. She kept paddling around in circles, thinking about the rock, and the duck, and his heart that might break.
<v Ben Loory>那天晚上，母鸭子辗转难眠。她一直在原地不停地划水，想着那块石头，想着那只鸭子，想着他那颗可能破碎的心。

00:40:34.480 --> 00:40:41.110
<v Ben Loory>She thought long and hard. And before morning, she had an idea. She went and woke up the strange duck.
<v Ben Loory>她想了很久。天亮前，她想到了一个主意。她去叫醒了那只奇怪的鸭子。

00:40:41.110 --> 00:40:46.770
<v Ben Loory>"Things happen when they must," she said, as if it were an extremely meaningful statement.
<v Ben Loory>“事情总会在必须发生时发生，”她说，仿佛那是一句非常有深意的话。

00:40:46.770 --> 00:40:49.000
<v Ben Loory>"So?" said the duck.
<v Ben Loory>“所以呢？”鸭子说。

00:40:49.000 --> 00:40:53.870
<v Ben Loory>"So I have a plan," said the girl duck, "and I think that it will work."
<v Ben Loory>“所以我有了一个计划，”母鸭子说，“而且我觉得这会管用。”

00:40:53.870 --> 00:40:58.240
<v Ben Loory>"Well, what is it?" said the duck, nearly bursting with excitement.
<v Ben Loory>“嗯，是什么计划？”鸭子兴奋得几乎要炸开了。

00:40:58.240 --> 00:41:12.910
<v Ben Loory>"We will need help," said the girl duck. "And it will take some time. And also, we will need a cliff."
<v Ben Loory>“我们需要帮忙，”母鸭子说。“这会花些时间。而且，我们需要一座悬崖。”

00:41:12.910 --> 00:41:21.760
<v Ben Loory>Two days later they set out. It took four ducks to carry the rock. They worked in teams and traded off every 15 minutes.
<v Ben Loory>两天后他们出发了。需要四只鸭子才能抬动那块石头。他们分队工作，每15分钟轮换一次。

00:41:21.760 --> 00:41:28.530
<v Ben Loory>Everyone joined in, even though they laughed, for ducks are all brothers when it comes right down to it.
<v Ben Loory>每个人都加入了，尽管他们都在笑，因为鸭子归根结底都是兄弟。

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:39.820
<v Ben Loory>"The cliff is over that hill and then quite a ways to the south," said the most elderly duck. "I remember flying over it when I was fledgling. It looked like the edge of the world."
<v Ben Loory>“悬崖在那座山后面，往南走很远，”最老的那只鸭子说。“我还是一只小鸟的时候飞过那里。它看起来像是世界的尽头。”

00:41:39.820 --> 00:41:46.160
<v Ben Loory>The ducks trudged on under their rocky weight for hours. For hours, and then for days.
<v Ben Loory>鸭子们带着石头的重量跋涉了数小时。数小时，然后是数天。

00:41:46.160 --> 00:41:51.440
<v Ben Loory>At night, they camped under hedges and strange trees, and ate beetles and frogs.
<v Ben Loory>晚上，他们在树篱和陌生的树木下露营，吃着甲虫和青蛙。

00:41:51.440 --> 00:41:55.760
<v Ben Loory>"Do you think it will be much farther?" said one of the ducks.
<v Ben Loory>“你觉得还要走很远吗？”其中一只鸭子说。

00:41:55.760 --> 00:42:04.200
<v Ben Loory>"Maybe," said the old duck. "My memory is not so good anymore."
<v Ben Loory>“也许吧，”老鸭子说。“我的记忆力没以前好了。”

00:42:04.200 --> 00:42:07.560
<v Ben Loory>On the sixth day, the ducks began to tire.
<v Ben Loory>第六天，鸭子们开始疲惫了。

00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:10.900
<v Ben Loory>"I don't believe there is a cliff," said one of them.
<v Ben Loory>“我不相信有悬崖，”其中一只说。

00:42:10.900 --> 00:42:15.420
<v Ben Loory>"Me neither," said another. "I think the old duck is crazy."
<v Ben Loory>“我也不信，”另一只说。“我觉得那只老鸭子疯了。”

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:19.410
<v Ben Loory>"My back hurts," said a third duck. "I want to go home."
<v Ben Loory>“我的背好疼，”第三只鸭子说。“我想回家。”

00:42:19.410 --> 00:42:23.210
<v Ben Loory>"Me too," said a fourth. "In fact, I'm going to."
<v Ben Loory>“我也是，”第四只说。“事实上，我正要回去。”

00:42:23.210 --> 00:42:33.880
<v Ben Loory>And then, all the ducks began to turn for home. The rock fell to the forest floor and lay there. The strange duck looked imploringly at the girl duck.
<v Ben Loory>接着，所有的鸭子都开始往回走。石头掉在森林的地面上。那只奇怪的鸭子恳求地看着母鸭子。

00:42:33.880 --> 00:42:37.410
<v Ben Loory>"Don't worry," she said. "I won't leave you."
<v Ben Loory>“别担心，”她说。“我不会离开你的。”

00:42:37.410 --> 00:42:44.030
<v Ben Loory>They watched all the other ducks flee homeward. And then they hoisted the rock onto their backs and trudged on.
<v Ben Loory>他们看着其他鸭子逃离回家。然后他们把石头抬到背上继续前进。

00:42:44.030 --> 00:42:48.350
<v Ben Loory>"What do you think will happen when we throw it off the cliff?" said the duck.
<v Ben Loory>“你觉得我们把它扔下悬崖时会发生什么？”鸭子说。

00:42:48.350 --> 00:42:54.760
<v Ben Loory>"I don't know," said the girl duck. "I just know it will be something."
<v Ben Loory>“我不知道，”母鸭子说。“我只知道那会发生点什么。”

00:42:54.760 --> 00:43:06.410
<v Ben Loory>Finally, they came to the edge of the cliff. The drop-off was so great they couldn't see the ground. Just great white clouds spread out before them, like an endless, rolling cotton blanket.
<v Ben Loory>终于，他们来到了悬崖边。落差太大了，他们看不见地面。只有巨大的白云铺展在他们面前，像一条无尽的、滚动的棉毯。

00:43:06.410 --> 00:43:09.680
<v Ben Loory>"It looks so soft," said the duck.
<v Ben Loory>“它看起来真柔软，”鸭子说。

00:43:09.680 --> 00:43:14.030
<v Ben Loory>"Yes, it does," said the girl duck. "Are you ready?"
<v Ben Loory>“是的，确实如此，”母鸭子说。“你准备好了吗？”

00:43:14.030 --> 00:43:17.250
<v Ben Loory>The duck looked at the rock.
<v Ben Loory>鸭子看着那块石头。

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:27.310
<v Ben Loory>"This is it, my love," he said, "the moment of truth. And whatever happens, please remember, always remember, I love you."
<v Ben Loory>“就是现在了，我的爱人，”他说，“真相大白的时刻。无论发生什么，请记住，永远记住，我爱你。”

00:43:27.310 --> 00:43:35.620
<v Ben Loory>And the two ducks hurled the rock off the cliff together.
<v Ben Loory>两只鸭子一起把石头从悬崖上扔了下去。

00:43:35.620 --> 00:43:44.950
<v Ben Loory>At first the rock simply fell "like a rock," one might say. "Like a stone." But then something began to happen.
<v Ben Loory>起初，石头只是“像石头一样”掉下去了，有人可能会说。“像一块石头。”但接着，有些事情开始发生。

00:43:44.950 --> 00:43:54.380
<v Ben Loory>It began to slow. It began to grow. It began to change. It narrowed. It elongated. And it also spread sideways.
<v Ben Loory>它开始减速。它开始生长。它开始变化。它变窄了。它变长了。而且它也向两侧延伸。

00:43:54.380 --> 00:43:57.570
<v Ben Loory>"It's becoming a bird," the girl duck said.
<v Ben Loory>“它正在变成一只鸟，”母鸭子说。

00:43:57.570 --> 00:44:16.210
<v Ben Loory>And it was. It was becoming a beautiful gray bird, really not that unlike a duck. Its wings began to move slowly up and down, up and down. And it dove down, and then coasted up. It looked back over its shoulder at the two ducks on the cliff, and it called out just once, "Good bye."
<v Ben Loory>确实如此。它变成了一只美丽的灰鸟，其实和鸭子没什么两样。它的翅膀开始缓慢地上下移动，上下移动。它俯冲下去，然后又滑翔而上。它回头看了看悬崖上的两只鸭子，只叫了一声：“再见。”

00:44:16.210 --> 00:44:30.160
<v Ben Loory>And then it was going, going, getting smaller and smaller, flying off over the blanket across the sky.
<v Ben Loory>然后它就走了，走了，变得越来越小，飞过了天空那层毯子。

00:44:30.160 --> 00:44:38.700
<v Ben Loory>When they reached the pond, the other ducks gathered around and clamored to hear what had happened. The duck and the girl duck glanced at each other.
<v Ben Loory>当他们回到池塘时，其他鸭子围了上来，吵着要听听发生了什么。鸭子和母鸭子对视了一眼。

00:44:38.700 --> 00:44:46.350
<v Ben Loory>"Nothing," said the girl duck. "It fell."
<v Ben Loory>“没什么，”母鸭子说。“它掉下去了。”

00:44:46.350 --> 00:45:00.650
<v Ben Loory>In the days that followed, the duck stayed to himself. The girl duck went and swan around in circles. She thought about that rocky bird flying off into the sky. She saw it over and over in her mind.
<v Ben Loory>在接下来的几天里，鸭子独自呆着。母鸭子游来游去。她想着那只飞向天空的石头鸟。她在脑海里一次又一次地回想它。

00:45:00.650 --> 00:45:10.310
<v Ben Loory>And then one day, not too many days later, she looked and saw the duck come swimming up. He was carrying a small salamander in his bill.
<v Ben Loory>然后有一天，没过几天，她看到那只鸭子游了过来。他嘴里叼着一只小蝾螈。

00:45:10.310 --> 00:45:12.970
<v Ben Loory>"For me?" the girl duck said.
<v Ben Loory>“给我的？”母鸭子说。

00:45:12.970 --> 00:45:21.770
<v Ben Loory>And the duck smiled.
<v Ben Loory>鸭子笑了。

00:45:21.770 --> 00:45:30.380
<v Ira Glass>Ben Loory, reading a story from his book, Stories for the Nighttime and Some for the Day.
<v Ira Glass>本·卢瑞，正在朗读他书中的故事，《夜间故事，以及一些白日故事》。

00:45:30.380 --> 00:45:33.900
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Fantastic Mr. Fox.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，神奇的狐狸先生。

00:45:33.900 --> 00:45:47.270
<v Ira Glass>When you're first falling for somebody, there is that period where you don't know if you should trust what is happening just yet, or should you trust them? And this question mark hangs over the whole thing. And part of what you're doing for love is gathering information to erase that question mark.
<v Ira Glass>当你第一次爱上某人时，有一个阶段，你不知道是否应该信任正在发生的事情，或者是否应该信任他们？这个问号悬在整件事上方。你为爱所做的一部分就是收集信息来消除那个问号。

00:45:47.270 --> 00:45:54.100
<v Ira Glass>Jeanne Darst met this guy, and thought of him as a fling and not serious dating material. At first, anyway.
<v Ira Glass>珍妮·达斯特遇到了这个家伙，起初把他当作艳遇，而不是严肃的约会对象。反正起初是这样。

00:45:54.100 --> 00:46:02.430
<v Ira Glass>Note to sensitive listeners. Once again, we refer to the existence of sex in this story, but give no details about how it works. Anyway, here's Jeanne.
<v Ira Glass>给敏感听众的备注。我们再次提到了性在这个故事中的存在，但没有详细说明它是如何运作的。总之，这就是珍妮。

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:06.940
<v Jeanne Darst>It was a pretty romantic set up. He was photographing me for a work thing.
<v Jeanne Darst>那是一个相当浪漫的安排。他在为一个工作项目给我拍照。

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:18.890
<v Jeanne Darst>My first impression of him was that he was a little silly, like a lot of men in Los Angeles are. Wearing goofy, expensive sneakers like a rich five-year-old and mispronouncing words. But also, super likable.
<v Jeanne Darst>我对他的第一印象是他有点傻，就像很多洛杉矶男人一样。像个有钱的五岁小孩一样穿着滑稽昂贵的运动鞋，发音不准。但也超级招人喜欢。

00:46:18.890 --> 00:46:33.110
<v Jeanne Darst>As we were talking about the layout for the shoot, he asked me, what do you think of monogamy? Later, when we had been dating a while, I asked him why he asked me what I thought of monogamy. And he said, because I wanted to date someone who wanted to be monogamous.
<v Jeanne Darst>当我们讨论拍摄方案时，他问我：“你对一夫一妻制怎么看？”后来，当我们约会了一段时间后，我问他为什么问我对一夫一妻制的看法。他说，因为我想和想实行一夫一妻制的人约会。

00:46:33.110 --> 00:46:45.360
<v Jeanne Darst>The second time we met, he asked me to his house for a margarita. When I told him I was sober, he said, that's amazing. I'm sober a lot of the time too. I haven't had a drink for eight days.
<v Jeanne Darst>我们第二次见面时，他约我去他家喝玛格丽特酒。当我告诉他我戒酒时，他说：“那太棒了。我很多时候也戒酒。我已经八天没喝酒了。”

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:56.720
<v Jeanne Darst>The ridiculousness of this was somehow exceedingly charming to me. Someone who, in trying to play down their drinking, has no idea that everything they're saying is screaming I'm an alcoholic.
<v Jeanne Darst>这种荒谬感不知为何对我来说极具魅力。一个试图掩饰自己饮酒习惯的人，根本不知道他所说的每一句话都在尖叫：“我是个酒鬼。”

00:46:56.720 --> 00:47:05.340
<v Jeanne Darst>Normal drinkers don't know how many days it has been since their last drink. In the way that, say, I couldn't tell you when I last had red potatoes.
<v Jeanne Darst>普通酒友不会记得自己最后一次喝酒是多少天前。就像，我没法告诉你我最后一次吃红薯是什么时候。

00:47:05.340 --> 00:47:23.010
<v Jeanne Darst>I guess I had thought that I would play in his power of now world for a week or two. That Jake and I would have fun, and that would be that. But things were quickly, well, better than that. We had a lot in common. He was divorcing like me, had a kid my kid's age. He understood having dinner at 5:30.
<v Jeanne Darst>我想我原本以为我会进入他那“当下”的力量世界里待上一两周。我和杰克会玩得很开心，仅此而已。但情况很快……好吧，比那更好。我们有很多共同点。他和我一样在闹离婚，有个和我孩子同龄的孩子。他理解在5:30吃晚饭的意思。

00:47:23.010 --> 00:47:42.280
<v Jeanne Darst>He was sexy, and sexy, and had good ideas about things to do. He'd call and say, let's take the boys to an outdoor movie at the golf course tonight. And when I'd be in the middle of a work thing and realize we were all going to starve, I'd get a text saying he was making sandwiches, and bringing blankets and pillows, and picking us all up.
<v Jeanne Darst>他很性感，性感，而且对要做的事情很有想法。他会打电话来说：“今晚带孩子们去高尔夫球场看露天电影吧。”而当我忙于工作、意识到我们都要饿坏了时，我会收到短信，说他正在做三明治，带着毯子和枕头，准备接我们所有人。

00:47:42.280 --> 00:47:52.340
<v Jeanne Darst>He called me Fox-- constantly. I forgave this because I thought it was a professional tick. And after my divorce, I wasn't looking for an argument.
<v Jeanne Darst>他不停地叫我“狐狸”（Fox）。我原谅了这点，因为我认为这是一种职业习惯。而且离婚后，我不想找茬。

00:47:52.340 --> 00:48:02.940
<v Jeanne Darst>I soon realized he called everyone Fox. Hey, Fox. My neighbors, his agent. He probably called his own mother Fox. What's up, Fox, how's the arthritis?
<v Jeanne Darst>我很快意识到他叫谁都是“狐狸”。嘿，狐狸。我的邻居，他的经纪人。他可能连他亲妈都叫狐狸。“怎么了，狐狸，关节炎怎么样了？”

00:48:02.940 --> 00:48:22.380
<v Jeanne Darst>My friends don't want to be called Fox. They really, really don't want to called Fox. But again, I let it ride because he worked in the fox industry, and had done a lot of drugs, and was pushing 50. Maybe he couldn't remember anyone's name.
<v Jeanne Darst>我的朋友们可不想被称为狐狸。她们真的很、真的很不想被称为狐狸。但再说一次，我让它顺其自然，因为他在狐狸行业工作，吸了很多毒，快五十岁了。也许他记不住任何人的名字。

00:48:22.380 --> 00:48:39.970
<v Jeanne Darst>Despite his stale vaudeville sleazy photographer routine, he seemed to be a lot more serious about getting serious than I was. After about a month and a half, he told me he was in love with me. I didn't say the same back because, while I was definitely crazy about him, something was off.
<v Jeanne Darst>尽管他有老掉牙的杂耍般油腻摄影师作派，但他似乎比我更认真地对待认真谈恋爱这件事。大约一个半月后，他告诉我他爱上我了。我没有回应，因为虽然我绝对为他疯狂，但有些地方不对劲。

00:48:39.970 --> 00:48:54.350
<v Jeanne Darst>For instance, I sensed that Jake needed to make a connection to other women as well-- everywhere we went. Like the sales lady at CVS. Who the hell cares about this person? Can we go? Do you really need her to think you're terrific?
<v Jeanne Darst>例如，我感觉到杰克也需要和别的女人建立联系——无论我们走到哪儿。就像CVS的销售小姐。谁会在乎这个人？我们能走吗？你真的需要她觉得你很棒吗？

00:48:54.350 --> 00:49:05.190
<v Jeanne Darst>A month later, we went to New York for work and Thanksgiving. And he went down to use the building's gym. Ad his journal was right there. Right there.
<v Jeanne Darst>一个月后，我们去纽约工作并过感恩节。他去楼下的健身房。他的日记就放在那儿。就在那儿。

00:49:05.190 --> 00:49:16.240
<v Jeanne Darst>I'd like to tell you that I had some hesitation about whether to open this thing up and read it. That I thought for even one single second about right and wrong. But I didn't.
<v Jeanne Darst>我很想告诉你们我当时犹豫过是否要打开这东西读一读。我哪怕一秒钟思考过对错。但我没有。

00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:38.480
<v Jeanne Darst>In his diary, there were some interesting entries. But let's see, the one that jumped out was Friday was Miho, Saturday was Kiku, Sunday was Jeanne. Miho and Kiku are clearly women's names. So again, that was Friday was Miho, Saturday was Kiku, Sunday was Jeanne.
<v Jeanne Darst>在他的日记里，有一些有趣的条目。来看看吧，跳入眼帘的是：“周五是美穗，周六是纪久，周日是珍妮。”美穗和纪久显然是女人的名字。所以再次强调，那是：“周五是美穗，周六是纪久，周日是珍妮。”

00:49:38.480 --> 00:49:48.760
<v Jeanne Darst>And then he wrote, "What's going on with me?" Three women in three days? What's going on with you? Well, a lot, apparently.
<v Jeanne Darst>然后他写道：“我这是怎么了？”三天内三个女人？你这是怎么了？好吧，显然发生了很多事。

00:49:48.760 --> 00:49:59.530
<v Jeanne Darst>And then I read that he did not have an attraction to-- page turn-- white women. White women like me.
<v Jeanne Darst>然后我读到他并不被——翻页——白人女性吸引。像我这样的白人女性。

00:49:59.530 --> 00:50:10.030
<v Jeanne Darst>I knew he had dated some Asian women and his ex-wife was Asian. He had Asian assistants, but I didn't think too much about it. I guess that's why I got Sunday.
<v Jeanne Darst>我知道他约会过一些亚洲女性，他的前妻也是亚洲人。他有亚洲助手，但我没太在意。我想这就是为什么我得到了周日。

00:50:10.030 --> 00:50:31.880
<v Jeanne Darst>Maybe it was my fault. I probably should have said right at the start of the relationship, I'm not Asian-- before anyone got hurt. Me. Before I got hurt.
<v Jeanne Darst>也许是我的错。我可能在关系开始时就应该说：“我不是亚洲人。”在有人受伤之前。我。在我受伤之前。

00:50:31.880 --> 00:50:57.550
<v Jeanne Darst>I felt like I had been thrown across the room. Delusion is one thing. Self-delusion is in my bones. But lying? Those aren't my people, liars. There's no style in lying. There's no honor, no panache. And there was no need. I was going way slower than he was, had, in fact, suggested to him that we keep things loose, see other people. But he insisted he did not want to see other people.
<v Jeanne Darst>我觉得我被狠狠地羞辱了。自欺欺人是一回事。自欺欺人刻在我的骨子里。但撒谎呢？那些不是我的人，撒谎者。撒谎毫无格调。没有荣誉，没有风度。而且完全没必要。我比他进展慢得多，事实上我曾建议他我们保持开放，见见其他人。但他坚持说他不想见其他人。

00:50:57.550 --> 00:51:02.270
<v Jeanne Darst>I then read in the journal, "There are some real red flags with Jeanne."
<v Jeanne Darst>我随后在日记里读到：“珍妮身上有一些真正的危险信号。”

00:51:02.270 --> 00:51:11.190
<v Jeanne Darst>Red flags, me. I've got red flags. I've been flagged. You're banging two other women on this page alone and you flag me?
<v Jeanne Darst>危险信号？我？我有危险信号？我被打上标记了。你光在这一页上就和另外两个女人鬼混，然后你标记我？

00:51:11.190 --> 00:51:20.550
<v Jeanne Darst>I decided then and there that I had just been handed my new stage name, Redd Flaggs-- two D's, two G's.
<v Jeanne Darst>我当即决定，我刚才收到了我的新艺名，雷德·弗拉格斯（Redd Flaggs）——两个D，两个G。

00:51:20.550 --> 00:51:33.770
<v Jeanne Darst>I stopped reading, and grabbed my jacket, and headed out to meet my friends. I texted him what I had done, and said I would get my things later. I got a text back saying, "Don't run, Jeanne. Talk."
<v Jeanne Darst>我停止阅读，抓起外套，出门去见我的朋友们。我发短信告诉他我做了什么，并说我会晚点去取东西。我收到回信说：“别跑，珍妮。谈谈。”

00:51:33.770 --> 00:51:50.880
<v Jeanne Darst>Don't run? Don't run? I can't put on my Nikes fast enough, Fox. I never talked to him again.
<v Jeanne Darst>别跑？别跑？我穿上耐克鞋的速度快得不能再快了，狐狸。我再也没跟他说过话。

00:51:50.880 --> 00:51:58.090
<v Jeanne Darst>The following morning, I drove to a house in the country for Thanksgiving with my sister, and her husband, and two kids, and a bunch of old friends.
<v Jeanne Darst>第二天早上，我开车去乡下的一栋房子和我的姐妹、她的丈夫、两个孩子以及一群老朋友共度感恩节。

00:51:58.090 --> 00:52:09.370
<v Jeanne Darst>I had woken up sick, and by the time the car pulled into the driveway, I had laryngitis and couldn't speak. So for the next two days, in this house in the woods, I couldn't say a word.
<v Jeanne Darst>我醒来时病了，等车开进车道时，我患上了喉炎，无法说话。所以接下来的两天，在这座林子里的房子里，我一句也说不出来。

00:52:09.370 --> 00:52:24.380
<v Jeanne Darst>I sat by the blazing fire outside, the white girl whose boyfriend didn't like white girls. Yes, he was a jerk. But I was a snoop. I now actually felt bad about what I'd done.
<v Jeanne Darst>我坐在燃烧的壁炉旁，那个男朋友不喜欢白人女性的白人女孩。是的，他是个混蛋。但我是个窥探者。我对自己做过的事现在真的感到难过了。

00:52:24.380 --> 00:52:31.830
<v Jeanne Darst>That night, everyone else took turns telling me their snooping stories, trying to cheer me up. My favorite was one from my sister, Liz.
<v Jeanne Darst>那天晚上，其他人轮流告诉我他们的偷窥故事，试图让我振作起来。我最喜欢的是我姐妹莉兹讲的故事。

00:52:31.830 --> 00:52:39.800
<v Liz>He started out-- it was one of those relationships where he started out super enthusiastic, like showing up at my office and calling all the time. And giving me presents, and really, really into it.
<v Liz>他刚开始的时候——那是一段关系，他刚开始时超级热情，就像出现在我的办公室并一直打电话。给我礼物，而且非常、非常投入。

00:52:39.800 --> 00:52:46.410
<v Jeanne Darst>This was back when she was about 24 and dating this musician. After four months, it went suddenly, unexpectedly cold.
<v Jeanne Darst>那是她大约24岁、和一个音乐家约会的时候。四个月后，他突然、出人意料地冷淡了下来。

00:52:46.410 --> 00:52:50.790
<v Liz>So I just wanted to find out what happened and why he changed his opinion.
<v Liz>所以我只是想找出发生了什么，为什么他改变了看法。

00:52:50.790 --> 00:53:10.870
<v Liz>I was at his apartment. He went into the shower, and he left his diary sitting out, which is something-- he was always writing in his diary. It was just sitting there, sort of burning a hole in the nightstand, as I was sitting on the bed. And I finally couldn't take it anymore. I just figured, I'll just take one little peek. Just to sort of see what he's thinking about. And you get that feeling when you're going out with someone, you don't really know them, and you're just trying to get to know them better.
<v Liz>我在他的公寓里。他进浴室洗澡，把日记放在那儿，那件事……他总是写日记。它就放在那儿，就在床头柜上烧出了一个洞，我就坐在床上。我终于忍无可忍了。我想：“我就看一眼。看看他在想什么。”当你和某人约会，不太了解他们，只想更好地了解他们时，你就会有那种感觉。

00:53:10.870 --> 00:53:41.660
<v Liz>So the first page I opened to I see what basically looks like a to-do list. And it's like, buy new guitar picks, get air conditioner fixed, call Grandma on her birthday, or something. And number four was break up with Liz. So it was just like the saddest-- it was the most mundane list of boring things that he eventually had to get around to doing. And I was the fourth on the list?
<v Liz>所以我打开的第一页，看到的看起来基本上是一张待办事项清单。上面写着：“买新吉他拨片，修理空调，在奶奶生日时给她打电话，”之类的。第四条是“和莉兹分手”。所以那简直是最悲伤的……那是他最终必须得做的最琐碎、最无聊的事情清单。而我排在清单的第四位？

00:53:41.660 --> 00:53:59.940
<v Liz>And then, I guess looking for more info, I just kind of quickly flipped through to see a couple things. And just, my eye-- your eye is just searching for your own name. And I saw something about like, problems with Liz. And one was like, Liz's boobs are too big. And I was like, what kind of person says that about someone? It wasn't like I was 80 years old, and I had like the world's saggiest ones. I was like, are you crazy?
<v Liz>然后，我想大概是寻找更多信息，我就快速翻了翻想看点什么。然后，我的眼睛……你的眼睛只是在搜索你自己的名字。我看到了一些关于“和莉兹的问题”。其中一个是像“莉兹的胸部太大”。我当时就想：“什么样的人会这样说别人？”又不是说我80岁了，我有世界上最下垂的胸部。我心想：“你疯了吗？”

00:53:59.940 --> 00:54:01.010
<v Jeanne Darst>She's too pretty.
<v Jeanne Darst>她太漂亮了。

00:54:01.010 --> 00:54:11.710
<v Liz>Yeah.
<v Liz>是啊。

00:54:11.710 --> 00:54:24.020
<v Jeanne Darst>After reading his diary, she ran home and called him up and broke up with him, beating him to the punch, basically. Liz isn't all that hung up on snooping being wrong. She just feels like it always bite you in the butt.
<v Jeanne Darst>读了他的日记后，她跑回家给他打电话并甩了他，基本上抢在他之前下手。莉兹并不觉得偷窥是错的。她只是觉得偷窥总是会反过来咬你一口。

00:54:24.020 --> 00:54:36.550
<v Jeanne Darst>And she's right. Reading Jake's journal did bite me in the butt. Some of what I read was so gross, there were unsavory details that I haven't talked about here. Details that I don't like having in my head.
<v Jeanne Darst>她说得对。读杰克的日记确实反咬了我一口。我读到的一些东西太恶心了，还有些我不喜欢在这里谈论的卑劣细节。我不喜欢自己脑子里有这些细节。

00:54:36.550 --> 00:54:52.120
<v Jeanne Darst>This is the high price of wanting to know the truth, you know the truth. And I don't want to snoop anymore. But it wasn't the dumbest thing I've ever done. It erased the question mark that hangs over every relationship at the beginning and gave me a clear answer.
<v Jeanne Darst>这就是想知道真相的高昂代价，你知道了真相。我不想再偷窥了。但这并非我做过最蠢的事。它消除了每段感情刚开始时悬而未决的问号，并给了我一个明确的答案。

00:54:52.120 --> 00:55:04.340
<v Jeanne Darst>It was horrible and devastating, and I'm glad I did it. I mean, without the proper intel, how was I supposed to know Jake was a liar? My sister, Liz, even liked him.
<v Jeanne Darst>这很可怕、很具毁灭性，但我很高兴我这么做了。我的意思是，没有适当的情报，我怎么能知道杰克是个骗子？我的姐妹莉兹甚至还喜欢他。

00:55:04.340 --> 00:55:13.720
<v Liz>He was just always like peppy and funny. And was like, huggy. And I don't know. Calling everyone beautiful, and sexy, and foxy.
<v Liz>他总是看起来充满活力、很有趣。而且像是……很喜欢拥抱。我不知道。叫每个人漂亮、性感、狐狸。

00:55:13.720 --> 00:55:14.690
<v Jeanne Darst>Ugh, Fox.
<v Jeanne Darst>呃，狐狸。

00:55:14.690 --> 00:55:15.030
<v Liz>Fox.
<v Liz>狐狸。

00:55:15.030 --> 00:55:15.760
<v Jeanne Darst>God.
<v Jeanne Darst>天哪。

00:55:15.760 --> 00:55:18.900
<v Liz>It's not so bad, is it?
<v Liz>没那么糟，对吧？

00:55:18.900 --> 00:55:25.640
<v Jeanne Darst>Yes.
<v Jeanne Darst>是的。

00:55:25.640 --> 00:55:30.280
<v Ira Glass>I got hurt. Redd Flaggs got hurt.
<v Ira Glass>我受伤了。雷德·弗拉格斯受伤了。

00:55:30.280 --> 00:55:54.890
<v Ira Glass>I don't think I'll ever read anyone's journal again. I never want to know what someone thinks of me in that kind of way ever again. It's too intense. I know I'm not Asian. I know I had reservations about Jake, instincts, which is why I read the journal.
<v Ira Glass>我想我再也不会读任何人的日记了。我再也不想知道别人那种对待我的想法了。这太强烈了。我知道我不是亚洲人。我知道我对杰克有保留意见，有直觉，这就是为什么我读了那本日记。

00:55:54.890 --> 00:56:06.010
<v Ira Glass>Jeanne Darst. She's the author of the memoir, Fiction Ruined My Family. She is single.
<v Ira Glass>珍妮·达斯特。她是回忆录《虚构毁了我的家庭》的作者。她目前单身。

